@inject ApiMetadata Api
@* Alert's Overview, written by hand after the selection and form field Overviews: status and
   variants, dismissing (previewed in each mode), an alert a post renders, and its labels.
   Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>An alert sits on the page, next to what it's about: the result of a form, or a warning above a table. For a message about the whole app, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/banner">Banner</Anchor>. For a short notice that doesn't need a place on the page, use a toast from the <Anchor Href="docs/components/toast-region">ToastRegion</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Status</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Status</Code> to say what the message means. It sets the color, the icon, and the word screen readers hear before the message.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="alert-statuses" />
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Icon</Code> to show a different icon, or <Code>HideIcon</Code> to show none.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Variants</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Variant</Code> to set the weight: <Code>Soft</Code> (the default), <Code>Outline</Code> or <Code>Solid</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="alert-variants" />

<Heading Level="2">Actions and dismissing</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Dismissible</Code> to add a close button. It works with JavaScript off too. To put buttons or links under the message, add an <Code>AlertActions</Code> at the end of the alert's content.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="alert-dismiss" />
<TextBlock>A closed alert stays closed only while the user stays on the page. It shows again:</TextBlock>
<List>
    <ListItem>after a reload, or on returning to the page;</ListItem>
    <ListItem>after any form post, with JavaScript off;</ListItem>
    <ListItem>after a form post on static pages, if the alert is inside the form that posted or its text has changed;</ListItem>
    <ListItem>on interactive pages, when <Code>Dismissed</Code> is set back to false.</ListItem>
</List>
<TextBlock>So an alert that reports a form post should be inside that form, or its text should say what happened ("Deleted Invoice 12"). Then the next post shows it again. On interactive pages, use <Code>@@bind-Dismissed</Code> to know when the user closes it.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>To keep a message closed on every page, use a Banner, which saves the choice in a cookie.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">After a form post</Heading>
<TextBlock>To say what a post did, render an alert from the submit handler. It arrives with the response, so it works in every render mode.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="alert-saved" />
<TextBlock>Put the saved value in the alert's text when you save, rather than binding the text to the field. On interactive pages the field changes as the user types, and the alert would change with it.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>On interactive pages, an alert the user has closed stays closed until <Code>Dismissed</Code> changes. The example binds <Code>Dismissed</Code> and sets it to false on each save, so every save shows the alert. On static pages the alert is inside the form that posted, so each response shows it again.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>StatusLabel</Code> to change the word screen readers hear before the message. The defaults are "Info", "Success", "Warning" and "Error", and none for <Code>Neutral</Code>; set it to <Code>""</Code> to leave it out. Use <Code>DismissLabel</Code> to change the close button's name (default "Dismiss"). To set them for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

@if (OverviewParts.Of(Component, Api).Count > 0)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Parts</Heading>
}
<OverviewParts Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <Alert Status="Status.Success" Title="Saved">Your changes are live.</Alert>

        @* A close button that works with JavaScript off too. *@
        <Alert Status="Status.Warning" Dismissible>Your trial ends in 3 days.</Alert>

        @* Buttons or links under the message, written last. *@
        <Alert Status="Status.Danger" Title="Couldn't save">
            The server didn't respond.
            <AlertActions><Button Size="ControlSize.Sm">Try again</Button></AlertActions>
        </Alert>
        """;
}
